# terraform/cloudflare

Zone-wide settings and the Terraform-owned DNS records for **{{ external_domain }}**.

The shape comes from `weisssrv-lib//terraform/modules/cloudflare-zone` at the
pinned ref in `main.tf`; the record inventory is `dns.tf`, and it is the only
file you normally edit.

## Zone settings

`main.tf` pins the zone's posture: strict TLS to the origin, always-use-HTTPS,
a 1.2 minimum TLS version, aggressive caching with a four-hour browser TTL, and
HSTS for one year.

HSTS is sent with `include_subdomains`, so it covers every name in the zone, not
only the ones this cluster serves. A browser that has seen one response refuses
plain HTTP for all of them until `max_age` expires. Backing that out means
setting `max_age = 0`, applying, and then waiting for the pins already issued to
run out. Preload stays off, which keeps the zone out of the browser preload
list.

Answer `external_domain` with a zone this cluster owns outright. This root also
publishes the apex records, the CAA set and a `p=reject` mail policy for it.

## Running it

```bash
task terraform:init     # once per checkout, and after a module ref bump
task terraform:plan
task terraform:apply
```

The tasks inject the API token, the account ID and the GitLab state backend
through `op run`, so nothing here needs a `.tfvars` file. State lives under this
project's GitLab-managed Terraform state, name `cloudflare`.

`terraform init` fetches the library module over HTTPS. If the library
repository is private, give git a credential for it first (`git credential`
helper, a `~/.netrc` entry, or an SSH module source).

## What Terraform owns, and what it does not

| Record class | Owner | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Apex, CAA, SPF/DMARC | Terraform (`dns.tf`) | Zone-level policy; nothing in-cluster can create it |
| Service names (`app.{{ external_domain }}`) | external-dns | Created from each IngressRoute, deleted with it |
| The apex address | The DDNS CronJob | Terraform seeds it once, then ignores `content` |

Adding a service record by hand here is how the two controllers start fighting:
external-dns owns a name for as long as its IngressRoute exists.

## Record map keys

Each key in the `dns_records` map in `dns.tf` is the record's Terraform state
address, and the per-record flags decide which resource it becomes.
`protected = true` routes it to a resource carrying `lifecycle.prevent_destroy`.
`content_managed_externally = true` stops Terraform diffing `content`, for a
record whose value another controller owns. Renaming a key or flipping a flag
plans a destroy-create, so read the next section first.

## Destroy protection

`protected = true` on a record means the module routes it to a resource carrying
`lifecycle.prevent_destroy`: removing the map entry then fails the plan instead
of silently deleting the record. Use it for anything whose deletion is an outage
or a security regression — the apex, any name carrying SSH or a VPN endpoint,
and the whole CAA set.

Because `lifecycle` blocks cannot take variables, the flag selects a *resource
address*: clearing it re-addresses the record from
`cloudflare_record.protected["<key>"]` to `cloudflare_record.this["<key>"]`,
which Terraform plans as a destroy and `prevent_destroy` refuses. Clearing the
flag on its own is the blocked step, so there are two paths.

- **Un-protecting a record you are keeping.** In one change, set
  `protected = false` AND add a root
  `moved { from = module.zone.cloudflare_record.protected["<key>"] to = module.zone.cloudflare_record.this["<key>"] }`.
  `moved` re-addresses state instead of destroying, so the apply is clean. Keep
  the `moved {}` block after the apply. It is the permanent record of the old
  state address, and removing one is a change of its own with its own plan and
  apply.
- **Deleting a record.** Do the un-protect change first, then drop the map entry
  in a second one: the record is an ordinary `cloudflare_record.this` by then
  and the destroy applies.

`prevent_destroy` does not block in-place updates. The CI apply refuses any saved
plan that deletes a resource. When the deletion is the reviewed intent, re-run
the manual `deploy-terraform` job with the job variable `ALLOW_DNS_DELETE=1`.

Pointing a real sender at this zone is the same kind of two-step: relax `spf`
and `dmarc` and add DKIM first, then tighten both back once alignment is clean.
An `rua` address outside this zone also needs
`{{ external_domain }}._report._dmarc` TXT `"v=DMARC1"` published in the RUA
domain (RFC 7489) before reporters will send; drop `rua` if you do not control
that domain.

## First apply

A fresh apply publishes `apex_seed_ip` (TEST-NET-1) until the DDNS CronJob's
next run, so the site is dark with a clean plan. Trigger the first run:

```bash
kubectl -n cloudflare-ddns create job --from=cronjob/cloudflare-ddns ddns-seed
```

## Credentials

| Item | Field | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `Cloudflare Terraform Token` | `credential` | Zone:Read + DNS:Edit + Zone Settings:Edit |
| `Cloudflare Terraform Token` | `username` | The account ID |
| `Git Terraform State Token` | `credential` | The GitLab state backend |

Keep the in-cluster DNS token (cert-manager, external-dns) as a *separate* item
with DNS:Edit + Zone:Read only. Zone Settings:Edit is what lets a token turn off
strict TLS for the whole domain; strict TLS and HSTS are asserted in `main.tf`.

## Changing DNS provider

`dns_backend` is an answer with one implementation. Everything provider-specific
is in this directory plus two consumers: the cert-manager `ClusterIssuer`'s
DNS-01 solver and the external-dns HelmRelease. A new backend needs a module
with the same three inputs (`zone_name`, credentials, a `records` map), an
issuer solver block, and an external-dns provider block —
The cluster template's `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` records the seam.
